content.thinking:
The user is asking about the death design - specifically what happens if you die again without picking up your tombstone. Let me trace through the code to understand the current behavior.

Looking at `playerDeath`:
```js
function playerDeath(){
  if(_dying)return;_dying=true;
  // 收集所有物品
  const items={};
  for(const k of Object.keys(W.inv))items[k]=(items[k]||0)+(W.inv[k]||0);
  for(const [slot,eq] of Object.entries(W.equipped)){if(eq)items[eq]=(items[eq]||0)+1;}
```

Now I'm storing the collected items and handling the death location differently based on whether you're in a dungeon or on the surface. If you're in a dungeon, I'm recording your position and floor number, placing a grave marker at your death spot, and caching that dungeon floor state.

Then I'm clearing out your inventory and equipment, resetting your health to half of maximum. But here's the thing—if you die again before retrieving your previous tombstone, the new death will overwrite your `deathItems` and `deathPos`, so you'd lose access to everything from your first death. I'm considering the design options here. The current behavior is pretty punishing—the first grave becomes permanently inaccessible when you die again. I could instead merge items into a single grave, explicitly design it as an intentional harsh mechanic with visual feedback, or stack everything into one active grave at a time. Let me trace through what the code actually does to understand the mechanics better. little or nothing—the old grave at (10,20) becomes orphaned since `collectDeathItems()` pulls from the current `W.deathItems` (now empty) and removes the grave at the new death position instead, leaving the old grave as a visual artifact that can't be collected.

I should explain this behavior to the user and ask whether they prefer a roguelike design where unrecovered items are permanently lost, or a more forgiving system that preserves them. Each grave could store its own item list instead of relying on a global deathItems variable, or I could keep it simpler with one active grave at a time where items merge together. This is more of a design choice than something requiring code changes right now.

